WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.167
(light ambient music)

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.124
- Hi, I'm Linda, I'm otherwise known

00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:09.719
as SaltySeattle, and I am a pasta artist.

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.899
I share most of my pasta
creations on Instagram

00:00:11.899 --> 00:00:14.158
because Instagram is such a perfect medium

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:18.090
for really visually arresting
and delicious things.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.703
Today we're making all of the colors

00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:21.716
for rainbow pappardelle,
and you go through

00:00:21.716 --> 00:00:23.204
the entire color of the rainbow.

00:00:23.204 --> 00:00:24.401
And it's not arbitrary,

00:00:24.401 --> 00:00:27.073
we continue to build on each new color.

00:00:27.073 --> 00:00:29.293
The first color of pasta dough
I'm going to make is blue.

00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:32.230
I use a special flower called
the butterfly pea flower,

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:34.019
and it grows on a vine
and it's bright blue,

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:36.020
and it's native to southeast Asia.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.396
To make purple pasta dough
I love to use a mixture

00:00:38.396 --> 00:00:42.199
of dragonfruit and
blueberry, and I like to use

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:47.081
double zero pasta flour
because the texture is supple,

00:00:47.081 --> 00:00:50.844
and it has a very low gluten
slash protein content.

00:00:50.844 --> 00:00:53.200
For the next pasta dough
color, it'll be red.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.218
And so I like to mix
together beets and harissa.

00:00:56.218 --> 00:00:58.134
And beets by themselves are really pink,

00:00:58.134 --> 00:00:59.874
and so you add the harissa
in there to sort of offset

00:00:59.874 --> 00:01:03.106
that pinkness and give it a
little bit more of a true red.

00:01:03.106 --> 00:01:06.836
Today I'm using harissa to
achieve my orange pasta dough,

00:01:06.836 --> 00:01:09.759
but you could also use
paprika to achieve this color.

00:01:09.759 --> 00:01:12.173
I use turmeric to make
the yellow pasta dough,

00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:13.700
and I love to use the whole

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.321
tumeric root rather than the powder.

00:01:15.321 --> 00:01:17.565
For the last pasta dough I'm
gonna go with bright green,

00:01:17.565 --> 00:01:18.885
and in order to achieve that bright green,

00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:20.990
I use parsley, and I
like to mix just a tiny

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.983
little bit of tumeric with it.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.722
So to make the rainbow we're
gonna do rainbow stripes

00:01:27.722 --> 00:01:30.677
and use this cutter for the pasta machine

00:01:30.677 --> 00:01:33.131
called a fettuccine
cutter to make our strips

00:01:33.131 --> 00:01:35.545
of different colors of pasta dough.

00:01:35.545 --> 00:01:38.010
Now we have our stripes
placed on our base sheet,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.813
and we're gonna run it back
through the pasta machine.

00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:43.225
If you don't have a pasta
machine and you want

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:46.033
to make pasta anyway,
just use a rolling pin.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.099
The cool thing about pasta
dough is you can re-roll it

00:01:48.099 --> 00:01:50.205
and re-roll it, and
you're not gonna hurt it

00:01:50.205 --> 00:01:52.368
as long as it doesn't dry out too much.

00:01:52.368 --> 00:01:55.096
I think I really honed my
pasta making skill, though,

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:58.819
living in Italy and just
learning from grandmas

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:02.427
and learning their specific
noodle that they were

00:02:02.427 --> 00:02:04.177
really passionate about.

00:02:04.177 --> 00:02:08.094
(classical instrumental music)

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.687
I'm making caramelle pasta,
and I'm using this really

00:02:18.687 --> 00:02:21.284
gorgeous magenta dough and combining it

00:02:21.284 --> 00:02:25.609
with a yellow, a bright tumeric
sunny yolk color yellow.

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:27.872
These tools that I'm using
right now are actually

00:02:27.872 --> 00:02:31.768
probably more common for
cake and cookie decorating.

00:02:31.768 --> 00:02:34.844
I always have pasta on
my mind and I'm always

00:02:34.844 --> 00:02:36.885
thinking about tools that I could

00:02:36.885 --> 00:02:39.315
potentially repurpose and use for pasta.

00:02:39.315 --> 00:02:42.174
The filling is made of ricotta
cheese and some nutmeg,

00:02:42.174 --> 00:02:45.010
salt, and a little bit of sugar.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.589
Caramelle means candies in
Italian, which you probably

00:02:47.589 --> 00:02:50.793
could have guessed just
by looking at their shape.

00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:53.626
(classical music)

00:02:57.571 --> 00:03:00.323
Pasta is a course, and
you do not serve anything

00:03:00.323 --> 00:03:02.730
with the pasta, it should
be loved and appreciated

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.302
and admired for itself as a course.

00:03:05.302 --> 00:03:08.056
The pasta shape we're making
now is called garganelli.

00:03:08.056 --> 00:03:11.146
They have ridges on them which
sauce just loves to cling to.

00:03:11.146 --> 00:03:12.426
This is a pasta bike.

00:03:12.426 --> 00:03:14.909
You can cut squares and
rectangles with this thing.

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:16.517
If you want to serve it with something

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:18.182
that's really hearty and substantial,

00:03:18.182 --> 00:03:20.698
then a thicker pasta could
really stand up to that.

00:03:20.698 --> 00:03:22.403
Really I would say that thinness

00:03:22.403 --> 00:03:25.320
is a matter of personal preference.

00:03:32.327 --> 00:03:34.726
I always grew up wanting to do art

00:03:34.726 --> 00:03:37.014
and it probably ultimately
came down to finding

00:03:37.014 --> 00:03:41.368
the right medium, and it
just feels right in my hands.

00:03:41.368 --> 00:03:44.701
(light classical music)

